<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Mar'ashi dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1359 CE – 1596 CE (approx.) · capital Amol</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of Shia sayyids that ruled Mazandaran on the Caspian for over two centuries, linked by marriage to the Safavid shahs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qavam-al-din-marashi">Sayyid Qavam al-Din Mar'ashi</a>, known as Mir-i Buzurg, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver</a> Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher whose dervish followers grew powerful in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>. In 1359 they defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/afrasiyabids-of-mazandaran">Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran</a>, and Qavam al-Din's sons established a theocratic principality based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sari-iran">Sari</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mar'ashis ruled as religious leaders as well as princes, spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver</a> Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the Caspian lands and backing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> in 1392 and deported the family, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> restored them. Later rulers were often divided among rival branches. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> married into the family: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, was a Mar'ashi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Despite these ties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> in 1596, ending Mar'ashi rule and making the province a royal domain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marashi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mar'ashi dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: محک · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marashiyan_government_1359-1582_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1359 CE – 1596 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amol</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qavam al-Din Mar'ashi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mazandarani, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mar'ashis, Marashi Sayyids</dd></dl></aside></div>
